1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,962
Here is a strange set.

2
00:00:01,962 --> 00:00:04,600
All the even prime
numbers bigger than two.

3
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:05,900
Four?

4
00:00:05,564 --> 00:00:07,690
Two times two, so not prime.

5
00:00:07,690 --> 00:00:08,590
Six?

6
00:00:08,204 --> 00:00:09,443
Two times three.

7
00:00:09,443 --> 00:00:10,343
Eight?

8
00:00:10,058 --> 00:00:11,247
Two times four.

9
00:00:11,247 --> 00:00:14,098
Every even number after two splits by two.

10
00:00:14,548 --> 00:00:18,515
Aarav checked a long list at his
study table and found none at all.

11
00:00:18,865 --> 00:00:20,388
So is this set nothing?

12
00:00:20,388 --> 00:00:21,678
Or is it something?

13
00:00:22,178 --> 00:00:23,412
It is something.

14
00:00:23,412 --> 00:00:25,268
A set with nothing inside.

15
00:00:25,268 --> 00:00:30,153
And it opens a whole family: empty,
finite, infinite and universal sets.

16
00:00:30,803 --> 00:00:32,353
Start with the empty set.

17
00:00:32,703 --> 00:00:36,119
A set that has no element at
all is called the empty set.

18
00:00:36,119 --> 00:00:39,122
It is also called the
null set, or the void set.

19
00:00:39,522 --> 00:00:41,755
We write it with this symbol, phi.

20
00:00:41,755 --> 00:00:44,774
Or as two curly brackets
with nothing between them.

21
00:00:45,174 --> 00:00:48,450
Watch the test run on even
primes bigger than two.

22
00:00:48,450 --> 00:00:49,457
Four is out.

23
00:00:49,457 --> 00:00:50,357
Six, out.

24
00:00:50,356 --> 00:00:52,556
Eight, ten, twelve, all out.

25
00:00:52,956 --> 00:00:56,244
The bag stays empty, and
the count stays at zero.

26
00:00:56,244 --> 00:00:58,787
The number of elements in phi is zero.

27
00:00:59,437 --> 00:01:03,050
More empty sets: the natural
numbers between one and two.

28
00:01:03,050 --> 00:01:05,446
The odd numbers whose square is four.

29
00:01:05,446 --> 00:01:07,538
Every test, and nothing passes.

30
00:01:08,138 --> 00:01:09,378
Side by side.

31
00:01:09,378 --> 00:01:11,871
The empty set is an empty box.

32
00:01:11,871 --> 00:01:13,157
Nothing inside.

33
00:01:13,557 --> 00:01:18,233
The set containing zero is a box with
one thing inside: the number zero.

34
00:01:18,233 --> 00:01:21,249
Zero is something, so
this set is not empty.

35
00:01:21,849 --> 00:01:23,010
Now you try.

36
00:01:23,010 --> 00:01:24,858
The set containing phi.

37
00:01:24,858 --> 00:01:26,875
Phi inside curly brackets.

38
00:01:26,875 --> 00:01:28,036
Is it empty?

39
00:01:28,036 --> 00:01:29,478
Pause and decide.

40
00:01:32,678 --> 00:01:33,593
Not empty!

41
00:01:33,593 --> 00:01:36,904
It is a box holding one
thing, an empty box.

42
00:01:36,904 --> 00:01:39,122
So it has exactly one element.

43
00:01:39,772 --> 00:01:41,922
Next, finite and infinite sets.

44
00:01:42,272 --> 00:01:46,565
A set is finite if it is empty, or
if you can count its elements and the

45
00:01:46,565 --> 00:01:48,184
counting comes to an end.

46
00:01:48,584 --> 00:01:50,112
The days of the week.

47
00:01:50,112 --> 00:01:51,916
Seven elements, so finite.

48
00:01:51,916 --> 00:01:53,434
The letters of SCHOOL.

49
00:01:53,434 --> 00:01:54,990
Five elements, finite.

50
00:01:55,390 --> 00:01:58,820
We write n of S for the number
of different elements in S.

51
00:01:59,220 --> 00:02:01,251
Now watch the natural numbers.

52
00:02:01,251 --> 00:02:02,668
One, two, three.

53
00:02:02,668 --> 00:02:03,729
Keep counting.

54
00:02:04,129 --> 00:02:05,161
A thousand.

55
00:02:05,161 --> 00:02:06,137
A million.

56
00:02:06,137 --> 00:02:07,114
A billion.

57
00:02:07,114 --> 00:02:09,650
The counter never reaches an end.

58
00:02:10,050 --> 00:02:12,768
A set that is not finite
is called infinite.

59
00:02:12,768 --> 00:02:17,441
The natural numbers, the integers, and
all the points on a line are infinite.

60
00:02:18,091 --> 00:02:21,498
In roster form we show the
pattern and add three dots.

61
00:02:21,498 --> 00:02:25,735
But the real numbers have no pattern
to list, so they need set builder

62
00:02:25,735 --> 00:02:26,635
form.

63
00:02:26,863 --> 00:02:27,763
Quick one.

64
00:02:27,762 --> 00:02:30,519
The natural numbers less than one crore.

65
00:02:30,519 --> 00:02:32,099
Finite, or infinite?

66
00:02:32,099 --> 00:02:33,370
Pause and think.

67
00:02:36,570 --> 00:02:37,470
Finite!

68
00:02:37,268 --> 00:02:41,678
The set is huge, but the counting
stops just before one crore.

69
00:02:41,678 --> 00:02:44,026
Big is not the same as endless.

70
00:02:44,676 --> 00:02:46,299
Last, the universal set.

71
00:02:46,649 --> 00:02:51,366
In any problem, all the sets we talk
about come from one big basic set.

72
00:02:51,366 --> 00:02:54,549
That basic set is the
universal set, written U.

73
00:02:54,949 --> 00:02:59,454
If our sets are the students who ride
a bicycle and the students who walk,

74
00:02:59,454 --> 00:03:02,679
the universal set is all
the students in the class.

75
00:03:03,079 --> 00:03:06,946
We draw U as a rectangle, and
every other set sits inside it.

76
00:03:07,596 --> 00:03:09,392
U depends on the problem.

77
00:03:09,392 --> 00:03:14,422
For sets of integers, U could be the
rational numbers, or the real numbers.

78
00:03:15,022 --> 00:03:16,159
Remember this.

79
00:03:16,159 --> 00:03:18,114
Empty means nothing inside.

80
00:03:18,114 --> 00:03:20,346
Finite means the counting ends.

81
00:03:20,346 --> 00:03:22,471
Infinite means it never does.

82
00:03:23,171 --> 00:03:24,906
Now, one worked example.

83
00:03:25,256 --> 00:03:29,685
Which of these sets are empty, which
are finite, and which are infinite?

84
00:03:29,685 --> 00:03:31,518
All four use natural numbers.

85
00:03:32,018 --> 00:03:32,918
One.

86
00:03:32,552 --> 00:03:36,226
Two x minus one equals zero
gives x equals one half.

87
00:03:36,226 --> 00:03:39,953
That is not a natural
number, so this set is empty.

88
00:03:40,703 --> 00:03:41,603
Two.

89
00:03:41,230 --> 00:03:44,527
x minus one, times x
minus two, equals zero.

90
00:03:44,527 --> 00:03:46,140
So x is one or two.

91
00:03:46,140 --> 00:03:48,093
Finite, with two elements.

92
00:03:48,843 --> 00:03:49,743
Three.

93
00:03:49,403 --> 00:03:53,599
The factors of twelve are one,
two, three, four, six and twelve.

94
00:03:53,599 --> 00:03:55,334
Finite, with six elements.

95
00:03:56,084 --> 00:03:56,984
Four.

96
00:03:56,594 --> 00:04:00,111
The prime numbers never run
out, so this set is infinite.

97
00:04:00,761 --> 00:04:02,226
Now, the exam trap.

98
00:04:02,576 --> 00:04:06,683
Many students write that the set
containing zero is the empty set.

99
00:04:07,083 --> 00:04:08,778
But zero is an element.

100
00:04:08,778 --> 00:04:11,441
The set containing zero has one element.

101
00:04:11,441 --> 00:04:13,446
Only the empty set has none.

102
00:04:13,446 --> 00:04:16,218
The same goes for the set containing phi.

103
00:04:16,868 --> 00:04:18,228
Where is this asked?

104
00:04:18,228 --> 00:04:22,544
Expect questions like: which of these
sets are empty, and which are finite

105
00:04:22,544 --> 00:04:23,444
or infinite?

106
00:04:24,029 --> 00:04:25,265
Exam level now.

107
00:04:25,265 --> 00:04:28,022
How many of these five sets are empty?

108
00:04:28,022 --> 00:04:29,311
Pause and solve.

109
00:04:34,711 --> 00:04:35,936
The answer is B.

110
00:04:35,936 --> 00:04:36,836
Three.

111
00:04:37,016 --> 00:04:37,916
A.

112
00:04:37,494 --> 00:04:40,555
No real number squared gives minus one.

113
00:04:40,555 --> 00:04:41,455
Empty.

114
00:04:41,767 --> 00:04:42,667
B.

115
00:04:42,229 --> 00:04:47,406
x squared minus three x plus two
is x minus one, times x minus two.

116
00:04:47,406 --> 00:04:49,218
So B is one and two.

117
00:04:49,218 --> 00:04:50,197
Not empty.

118
00:04:50,697 --> 00:04:51,597
C.

119
00:04:51,144 --> 00:04:54,399
No integer lies strictly
between one and two.

120
00:04:54,399 --> 00:04:55,299
Empty.

121
00:04:55,065 --> 00:04:55,965
D.

122
00:04:55,513 --> 00:04:58,440
The square root of two is not rational.

123
00:04:58,440 --> 00:04:59,340
Empty.

124
00:04:59,606 --> 00:05:02,576
E holds one element, the empty set itself.

125
00:05:02,576 --> 00:05:03,476
Not empty.

126
00:05:03,458 --> 00:05:06,286
So three sets are empty: A, C and D.

127
00:05:06,886 --> 00:05:08,345
Here is your recap.

128
00:05:08,345 --> 00:05:09,844
Screenshot this card.

129
00:05:13,044 --> 00:05:15,881
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

130
00:05:16,281 --> 00:05:17,181
One.

131
00:05:16,797 --> 00:05:19,247
Give two examples of the empty set.

132
00:05:20,147 --> 00:05:21,047
Two.

133
00:05:20,637 --> 00:05:24,164
Is the set of all points on
a line finite or infinite?

134
00:05:25,064 --> 00:05:25,964
Three.

135
00:05:25,693 --> 00:05:28,560
Why is the set containing zero not empty?

136
00:05:29,660 --> 00:05:32,880
Next time: Diya studies
three subjects for NEET.

137
00:05:32,880 --> 00:05:37,256
Every one of them is a PUC subject,
but not the other way round.

138
00:05:37,256 --> 00:05:39,409
We meet subsets and equal sets.
